  "response": "Baumeister and colleagues introduced the ego-depletion hypothesis in a 1998 study involving radishes and chocolate cookies, and that hypothesis proposed that self-control draws on a limited resource that becomes depleted with use. Hagger and colleagues published a 2010 meta-analysis that reported a medium-sized ego-depletion effect twelve years after the study. In the intervening period the paradigm spread rapidly. Laboratories adapted the original design to attention-regulation tasks, emotion suppression, Stroop interference, and handgrip persistence, and the resource model became one of the most frequently cited accounts in social psychology.\n\nBecause the Hagger synthesis reported an effect of medium size, it estimated the ego-depletion effect at roughly d = 0.62, a figure that many treated as a benchmark for the field. The 2010 Hagger meta-analysis included fewer than 20 independent tests of ego depletion. For a time, that synthesis appeared to put the resource model on firm footing. Its strength depended, however, on the representativeness of the studies it aggregated, and critics soon focused on how that body of work had been produced and filtered. At least one of two distortions, perhaps both, shaped the record: the published ego-depletion literature was inflated by publication bias, or it was inflated by flexible analytic practices. Although the 2010 estimate of roughly d = 0.62 had seemed robust, the publication bias in that literature confirms what Carter and colleagues found in 2015: bias-corrected meta-analytic estimates of ego depletion were close to zero. The gap between the uncorrected and corrected figures was too large to be dismissed as sampling noise. It meant that the apparent effect could plausibly be explained by which results reached print and how they were analysed.\n\nMeta-analytic correction can only adjust for bias after the fact, and its methods rest on contestable modelling assumptions. The decisive test therefore required new data collected under constraints that ruled out selective reporting from the outset. The 2016 Registered Replication Report pooled data from 23 laboratories. In other words, twenty-three independent labs contributed data to the 2016 Registered Replication Report, whereas the report collected data from 50 laboratories. Each participating site followed a common protocol that specified the depleting task, the dependent measure, the exclusion criteria, and the analysis plan before any participant was tested. This preregistration removed the analytic degrees of freedom that critics had blamed for the earlier literature, and the multisite structure ensured that null results from individual labs could not be quietly set aside.\n\nThe pooled result is what made the report consequential. Either the pooled confidence interval for the ego-depletion effect in the 2016 Registered Replication Report included zero, or it excluded zero; these two outcomes cannot both hold, and one of them must. The interval's inclusion of zero confirms the report's own conclusion that the overall ego-depletion effect was statistically indistinguishable from zero. That conclusion settled the tension between the once-celebrated figure of roughly d = 0.62 and the near-zero corrected estimates. Under conditions free of selective publication and flexible analysis, the medium-sized effect did not reappear, and the near-zero corrected estimates proved the more accurate guide to the phenomenon.\n\nProponents of the resource model questioned whether the replication task had been sufficiently depleting, and they organized further multisite work using protocols they themselves endorsed. By contrast with the 2016 report's null conclusion, the 2021 Vohs multisite study estimated the ego-depletion effect at about d = 0.5. The broader methodological lesson nonetheless stands. A literature built on small samples, unregistered analyses, and selective publication can produce a stable-looking meta-analytic effect that independent, preregistered, multisite designs fail to reproduce. For that reason, the replication efforts transformed ego depletion from a presumed fact about self-control into a cautionary case for how the field evaluates evidence.",
